m resources (matter, energy, information) whose preparation cost is polynomial in the description length of an organism, outputs an element of $\mathcal{S}$. Call the procedure \emph{iterative} if it consists of a sequence of generate-and-test steps (as in Darwinian variation and selection) or of a developmental/growth trajectory whose length scales at least polynomially with the description length. Call it \emph{non-iterative} if it produces a candidate in $\mathcal{S}$ in $O(1)$ physical steps after preparation.\n\nQuestion: Does there exist a physical (in particular, possibly quantum-mechanical) non-iterative search procedure that, with non-negligible probability, outputs an element of $V$? Concretely, can one prepare a quantum state $|\psi\rangle$ on a Hilbert space whose computational basis is indexed by $\mathcal{S}$, using only resources polynomial in the description length, such that a single projective measurement in that basis yields an outcome in $V$ with probability bounded below by an inverse polynomial? Or must any physical procedure achieving non-negligible success probability necessarily include an iterative preparation stage of length growing with the description length of viable organisms?","status_search_result":"The question is posed as an explicitly open conceptual problem at the interface of quantum physics, theoretical biology, and search complexity. No published mathematical or physical result is known to settle whether viable biological configurations can be reached by a genuinely non-iterative quantum process; standard quantum search lower bounds (e.g.\ BBBV) suggest unstructured search of a Vast space cannot be done non-iteratively, but no formal model of biological viability has been shown to fall under such bounds. Status remains open.","status_evidence":[{"title":"Quantum lower bounds by polynomials (BBBV / Beals et al.)","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9802049","snippet":"Establishes Omega(sqrt(N)) query lower bounds for unstruct